In the norm (4.5) the factor $(1-t)^{1/4}$ is the inverse of the rate (4.4) of $u^*$, and the other factor is $e^{\kappa s}$ in the self-similar time $s=-\log(1-t)$: up to a constant, a map in $C_\kappa([0,1);X)$ is smaller than $u^*$ by the factor $e^{-\kappa s}$. For a continuous $u:[0,1)\to X$ the equation (2.4) on $[0,1)$ reads $u=e^{t\Delta}u(0)+\mathcal B(u,u)$. Since $u^*$ satisfies it and $\mathcal B$ is symmetric, $u^*+v$ satisfies it, for a continuous $v:[0,1)\to X$, if and only if $$\begin{equation}
v=e^{t\Delta}v(0)+2\,\mathcal B(u^*,v)+\mathcal B(v,v).\tag{4.7}
\end{equation}$$ Let $g$ be the source in the linear problem (4.8). Undoing each period’s dilation and isometry gives the endpoints of $v-g$ in the original coordinates. Their norms must be $O(\Theta^n)$, where $\Theta=\lambda^\kappa\in(\lambda^{1/4},1)$. On the spectral subspace inside $|z|=\Theta$, forward iteration of the monodromy gives this decay. Outside the circle even a decaying mode can be too slow. There are only finitely many such directions, counting multiplicities, and summing the future forcing backwards determines their initial values.

